+// ---- where pipeline compiles run (the rules, and their host test, are in CompileQueue.h) ------------------------------------
+// PanVK compiles a pipeline's shaders on the thread that asks for it: one of iOS 27's compute shaders is 24 522 NIR instructions
+// (25 s of a Mali-G72's CPU) and a Preferences fragment shader never ended. #396 moved every compile to a worker and timed the
+// synchronous callers out after 5 s; on phone 2 (19 Nov) that refused backboardd's first render pipelines on a cold cache and
+// QuartzCore aborted on the refusal (launchd rebooted after three). So now:
+//  - the synchronous creation calls compile on the calling thread, every time, and return the pipeline or the compile's own
+//    error (gCompiler.sync): no timeout, no slot, no negative cache. A compile that would never end is ended by PanVK's compile
+//    budget (STARBITE_PANVK_COMPILE_BUDGET_MS of the compiling thread's CPU time), as a real compile error;
+//  - the completion-handler calls return at once and compile on a worker (gCompiler.async, at most 3 at a time, FIFO); the
+//    handler is called once, on the worker, with what the synchronous call would have returned. The worker queue has no QoS of
+//    its own, so a compile runs at the QoS of the thread that asked for it (on this kernel utility/background run on the A55s).
+//    An asynchronous compile that failed after more than 10 s is not started again for 10 minutes (the handler gets that error).
+// What makes a pipeline cheap is the shader cache (one per user, warmed by fullroot/steps.d/81-m2v-cache.sh), not a timer.
